@BaldingsWorld Of course they’re going to seek to. I addressed why in chips it will be very difficult to do so without demand.

I've also proposed significantly stricter controls on SME (which are also much easier to enforce).

https://t.co/LKMRMoYWyr

@hu_yifei hey yifei, with all due respect i'd appreciate if you didn't do ad-hominem attacks on our team. 

you're certainly welcome to criticize the benchmark as much as you'd like, disagree with fundamentals, setup, results, etc.

but we do have respect for you and your research team, and would ask for the same in return

@hashiromer621 @xujustinj We, nor anyone else in this space, can't avoid (2). 

I'm still confused why (1) is an issue though. There are concrete advantages to deterministic, repeatable metrics as long as their designed towards human prefs - if you look at many/most LLM benchmarks the metrics/reward functions are deterministic! 

we can agree to disagree, but i fundamentally don't believe a single-shot LLM as a judge with a singular metric is a better approach

@hashiromer621 @xujustinj i agree that annotation is hard! we're working on ways to help bootstrap annotation.

i also agree that LLM as a judge can be easier (we've done it quite a bit ourselves for 'quick' testing). 

ultimately there's no free lunch though. LLM as a judge can lack granularity and rigor for evaluation, and also it can be quite expensive to run repeatably if you're scaling up to thousands of docs / comparing against multiple solutions

